rly, cos\u00b2x:\n\n[\u221a2(\u221a3 + 1)/4]^2 = (2)( (\u221a3 + 1)^2 ) /16 = (2)(3 + 2\u221a3 + 1)/16 = (2)(4 + 2\u221a3)/16 = (8 + 4\u221a3)/16 = (2 + \u221a3)/4\n\nSo sin\u00b2x = (2 - \u221a3)/4 and cos\u00b2x = (2 + \u221a3)/4.\n\nNow, sin\u2074x and cos\u2074x can be computed by squaring sin\u00b2x and cos\u00b2x.\n\nsin\u2074x = [ (2 - \u221a3)/4 ]\u00b2 = (4 - 4\u221a3 + 3)/16 = (7 - 4\u221a3)/16\n\ncos\u2074x = [ (2 + \u221a3)/4 ]\u00b2 = (4 + 4\u221a3 + 3)/16 = (7 + 4\u221a3)/16\n\nSo sin\u2074x = (7 - 4\u221a3)/16, cos\u2074x = (7 + 4\u221a3)/16.\n\nNow let's compute each term under the square roots.\n\nFirst term under the first square root: sin\u2074x + 4cos\u00b2x\n\n= (7 - 4\u221a3)/16 + 4*(2 + \u221a3)/4\n\nWait, 4cos\u00b2x is 4*(2 + \u221a3)/4 = (2 + \u221a3). So:\n\nsin\u2074x + 4cos\u00b2x = (7 - 4\u221a3)/16 + (2 + \u221a3)\n\nConvert (2 + \u221a3) to sixteenths to add:\n\n= (7 - 4\u221a3)/16 + (32 + 16\u221a3)/16 = [7 - 4\u221a3 + 32 + 16\u221a3]/16 = (39 + 12\u221a3)/16\n\nSimilarly, the second term under the square root: cos\u2074x + 4sin\u00b2x\n\n= (7 + 4\u221a3)/16 + 4*(2 - \u221a3)/4\n\nSimilarly, 4sin\u00b2x = 4*(2 - \u221a3)/4 = (2 - \u221a3)\n\nSo:\n\ncos\u2074x + 4sin\u00b2x = (7 + 4\u221a3)/16 + (2 - \u221a3)\n\nConvert (2 - \u221a3) to sixteenths:\n\n= (7 + 4\u221a3)/16 + (32 - 16\u221a3)/16 = [7 + 4\u221a3 + 32 - 16\u221a3]/16 = (39 - 12\u221a3)/16\n\nSo now, the function f(x) becomes:\n\n\u221a( (39 + 12\u221a3)/16 ) - \u221a( (39 - 12\u221a3)/16 )\n\nSimplify the square roots:\n\n= [\u221a(39 + 12\u221a3) - \u221a(39 - 12\u221a3)] / 4\n\nSo now the problem reduces to computing \u221a(39 + 12\u221a3) and \u221a(39 - 12\u221a3), then subtracting them and dividing by 4.\n\nHmm, this seems tricky. Maybe these expressions can be simplified by expressing them as \u221aa \u00b1 \u221ab. Let me assume that \u221a(39 + 12\u221a3) can be written as \u221aa + \u221ab, where a and b are positive numbers. Then:\n\n(\u221aa + \u221ab)\u00b2 = 