 63 + 12 + 2 = 1973\n     \\]\n   - Between \\( n = 7899 \\) (1971 trailing zeros) and \\( n = 7900 \\) (1973 trailing zeros), there is a jump of 2, skipping 1972.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n- There exists an \\( n \\) (specifically 7895) such that \\( n! \\) has 1971 trailing zeros.\n- There is no \\( n \\) such that \\( n! \\) has 1972 trailing zeros.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Yes} \\text{ for 1971 trailing zeros}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{No} \\text{ for 1972 trailing zeros}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "To determine if there exists an $n$ such that the factorial $n!$ ends with exactly 1971 or 1972 zeros, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Understanding the Number of Trailing Zeros**:\n   The number of trailing zeros at the end of $n!$ is determined by the number of times $10 = 2 \\cdot 5$ is a factor in $n!$. Since there are generally more factors of 2 than factors of 5 in $n!$, the number of trailing zeros is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the factorial.\n\n2. **Counting Factors of 5 in $n!$**:\n   The number of times 5 is a factor in $n!$ can be calculated using:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{625} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n   \\]\n   where $\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor$ denotes the floor function, which takes the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$.\n\n3. **Calculating Specific Values**:\n   - Let us first pick $n = 8000$ and count the factors of 5 in $8000!$:\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{8000}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 1600\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{8000}{25} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 320 \\right\\rfloor = 320\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{8000}{125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 64 \\right\\rfloor = 64\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{8000}{625} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 12.8 \\right\\rfloor = 12\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{8000}{3125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 2.56 \\right\\rfloor = 2\n     \\]\n     Adding these up, the total number of factors of 5 is:\n     \\[\n     1600 + 320 + 64 + 12 + 2 = 1998\n     \\]\n     Thus, $8000!$ has 1998 trailing zeros.\n\n4. **Searching for the Exact Number**:\n   - To achieve exactly 1971 trailing zeros, we need to reduce the factors of 5. For instance, by subtracting multiples of 5, we effectively decrease the count of contributing factors.\n   - Let\u2019s try $n = 7900$:\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7900}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 1580\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7900}{25} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 316 \\right\\rfloor = 316\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7900}{125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 63.2 \\right\\rfloor = 63\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7900}{625} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 12.64 \\right\\rfloor = 12\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7900}{3125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 2.528 \\right\\rfloor = 2 \n     \\]\n     Adding these up:\n     \\[\n     1580 + 316 + 63 + 12 + 2 = 1973\n     \\]\n\n   - We need one more test case, let\u2019s further reduce by considering $n = 7895$. Counting the factors:\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7895}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 1579\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7895}{25} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 315.8 \\right\\rfloor = 315\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7895}{125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 63.16 \\right\\rfloor = 63\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7895}{625} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 12.632 \\right\\rfloor = 12\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\left\\lfloor \\frac{7895}{3125} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 2.528 \\right\\rfloor = 2\n     \\]\n\n     Summing these factors:\n     \\[\n     1579 + 315 + 63 + 12 + 2 = 1971\n     \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   We see that $7895!$ ends with 1971 zeros and there does indeed 