30^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{4 - 1}}{2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}} = \\sqrt{3}\n     \\]\n\n8. Since:\n   - \\(OO'' = FO' = \\frac{1}{2} AB = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n\n9. Thus, the radius of the circumcircle is:\n   \\[\n   OA = \\sqrt{AO''^2 + O''O^2} = \\sqrt{3 + \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{3 + \\frac{1}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n10. Finally, the volume of the circumscribed sphere is:\n    \\[\n    V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\right)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\cdot \\frac{13 \\sqrt{13}}{8} = \\frac{13 \\sqrt{13}}{6} \\pi\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{13 \\sqrt{13}}{6} \\pi}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a $10 \\times 10$ table, 100 distinct numbers are written. In one move, you can select any rectangular block of cells and rearrange all the numbers in it symmetrically relative to its center (\"rotate the rectangle by $180^{\\circ}$\"). Can you always arrange the numbers so that they increase in each row from left to right and in each column from bottom to top in 99 moves?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if it's possible to sort a 10x10 table of distinct numbers using at most 99 moves, where each move is rotating a rectangular block 180 degrees. The goal is to have the numbers increasing from left to right in each row and from bottom to top in each column. Hmm, let's start by understanding the problem.\n\nFirst, rotating a rectangle 180 degrees means that each cell in the rectangle is swapped with the cell diagonally opposite relative to the center of the rectangle. For example, in a 3x3 rectangle, the top-left cell swaps with the bottom-right cell, the top-middle swaps with the bottom-middle, and so on. This operation is essentially reversing the order of elements in both rows and columns within the selected rectangle.\n\nNow, the question is whether any permutation of 100 distinct numbers ca