2\u03c6 d\u03c6. The integral of cos2\u03c6 d\u03c6 is (1/2)sin2\u03c6. Evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0: (1/2)(sin4\u03c0 - sin0) = 0 - 0 = 0.\n\n3. \u222b\u2080\u00b2\u03c0 cos\u00b22\u03c6 d\u03c6. Use the identity cos\u00b2x = (1 + cos2x)/2. So, cos\u00b22\u03c6 = (1 + cos4\u03c6)/2. Therefore, integral becomes:\n\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2\u03c0 (1 + cos4\u03c6)/2 d\u03c6 = (1/2) \u222b\u2080\u00b2\u03c0 1 d\u03c6 + (1/2) \u222b\u2080\u00b2\u03c0 cos4\u03c6 d\u03c6\n\nCompute each part:\n\n(1/2)(2\u03c0) + (1/2)(0) = \u03c0 + 0 = \u03c0. Because the integral of cos4\u03c6 over 0 to 2\u03c0 is (1/4)sin4\u03c6 evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0, which is 0.\n\nTherefore, \u222b\u2080\u00b2\u03c0 cos\u00b22\u03c6 d\u03c6 = \u03c0.\n\nPutting it all back into the total area:\n\n(1/8)[2\u03c0 - 2*0 + \u03c0] = (1/8)(3\u03c0) = 3\u03c0/8.\n\nTherefore, the total area is 3\u03c0/8. If this area is for two petals, then each petal has area (3\u03c0/8)/2 = 3\u03c0/16. But wait, let's confirm if this is correct.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought each petal is traced over an interval of \u03c0. If that's the case, then maybe the area of one petal is (1/2) \u222b\u2080^\u03c0 [sin\u00b2\u03c6]^2 d\u03c6. Let's compute that and see if it equals 3\u03c0/16.\n\nCompute A_petal = (1/2) \u222b\u2080^\u03c0 sin\u2074\u03c6 d\u03c6.\n\nUse the power-reduction formula for sin\u2074\u03c6:\n\nsin\u2074\u03c6 = (3/8) - (1/2)cos2\u03c6 + (1/8)cos4\u03c6.\n\nTherefore, integral becomes:\n\n(1/2) \u222b\u2080^\u03c0 [3/8 - (1/2)cos2\u03c6 + (1/8)cos4\u03c6] d\u03c6\n\n= (1/2)[ (3/8)\u03c6 - (1/4)sin2\u03c6 + (1/32)sin4\u03c6 ] from 0 to \u03c0\n\nEvaluate at \u03c0:\n\n(3/8)\u03c0 - (1/4)sin2\u03c0 + (1/32)sin4\u03c0 = (3/8)\u03c0 - 0 + 0 = 3\u03c0/8\n\nEvaluate at 0: 0 - 0 + 0 = 0\n\nTherefore, A_petal = (1/2)(3\u03c0/8) = 3\u03c0/16.\n\nWait, but earlier the total area was 3\u03c0/8, which is twice 3\u03c0/16. Therefore, that seems consistent. So, integrating over 0 to \u03c0 gives 3\u03c0/16, which is one