\\varphi\n   \\]\n5. Compute each integral separately:\n   - \\(\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{3}{8} \\, d\\varphi = \\frac{3}{8} \\pi\\)\n   - \\(\\int_{0}^{\\pi} -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2\\varphi \\, d\\varphi = 0\\)\n   - \\(\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{8} \\cos 4\\varphi \\, d\\varphi = 0\\)\n6. Combine the results:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{3}{8} \\pi \\right) = \\frac{3}{16} \\pi\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of one petal of the curve \\(\\rho = \\sin^2 \\varphi\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{3\\pi}{16}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the area of one petal of the curve \\(\\rho = \\sin^2 \\varphi\\), we need to integrate in polar coordinates. Here's the detailed step-by-step solution:\n\n1. Determine the range for \\(\\varphi\\). Given the symmetry of the curve, one petal is traversed as \\(\\varphi\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. The area \\(S\\) of one petal in polar coordinates is given by the integral:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\rho^2 \\, d\\varphi.\n   \\]\n   \n3. Substitute \\(\\rho = \\sin^2 \\varphi\\) into the integral:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} (\\sin^2 \\varphi)^2 \\, d\\varphi = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin^4 \\varphi \\, d\\varphi.\n   \\]\n\n4. Use the double-angle identity to express \\(\\sin^4 \\varphi\\) in a more integrable form:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^4 \\varphi = \\left(\\sin^2 \\varphi\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\varphi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4} (1 - 2\\cos 2\\varphi + \\cos^2 2\\varphi).\n   \\]\n\n5. Next, use the identity for \\(\\cos^2\\theta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos^2 2\\varphi = \\frac{1 + \\cos 4\\varphi}{2}.\n   \\]\n   \n6. Substitute this back into the integral:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^4 \\varphi = \\frac{1}{4} \\left(1 - 2\\cos 2\\varphi + \\frac{1 + \\cos 4\\varphi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{3}{2} - 2\\cos 2\\varphi + \\frac{\\cos 4\\varphi}{2}\\right).\n   \\]\n\n7. Simplify the integrand:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^4 \\varphi = \\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{3}{2} - 2\\cos 2\\varphi + \\frac{\\cos 4\\varphi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{3}{8} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\varphi + \\frac{1}{8}\\cos 4\\varphi.\n   \\]\n\n8. Substitute this expression back into the integral:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\left(\\frac{3}{8} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\varphi + \\frac{1}{8}\\cos 4\\varphi\\right) d\\varphi.\n   \\]\n\n9. Integrate each term separately:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{3}{8} \\, d\\varphi - \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2\\varphi \\, d\\varphi + \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{8} \\cos 4\\varphi \\, d\\varphi\\right].\n   \\]\n\n10. Compute each integral:\n    \\[\n    \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{3}{8} \\, d\\varphi = \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\pi,\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2\\varphi \\, d\\varphi = \\left. \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 2\\varphi \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} = 0,\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\frac{1}{8} \\cos 4\\varphi \\, d\\varphi = \\left. \\frac{1}{32} \\sin 4\\varphi \\right|_{0}^{\\pi} = 0.\n    \\]\n\n11. Add the results:\n    \\[\n    S = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{3}{8} \\pi + 0 + 0 \\right) = \\frac{3\\pi}{16}.\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3\\pi}{16}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "1152 schoolchildren are participating in a tennis tournament. A player receives 1 point for a win and 0 points for a loss. Before each round, pairs are randomly formed from players with the same number of points (those who cannot be paired receive a point without playing). After the second loss, a player is eliminated from the tournament. The tournament continues as long as at least one pair of opponents can be formed. How many rounds will need to be held?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out how many rounds are needed in this tennis tournament with 1152 schoolchildren. The rules are a bit specific, so I need to parse them carefully. Let me start by restati