s(4t), \\\\\nz(t) &= e^{-2t} - 2 \\sin(4t).\n\\end{aligned}\n}\n$$", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the Cauchy problem for the given system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{d x}{d t}=8 y \\\\\n\\frac{d y}{d t}=-2 z \\\\\n\\frac{d z}{d t}=2 x+8 y -2 z\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith initial conditions \\( x(0)=-4 \\), \\( y(0)=0 \\), and \\( z(0)=1 \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Write the system in matrix form**:\n   $$\n   \\frac{d}{dt}\n   \\begin{bmatrix}\n   x \\\\\n   y \\\\\n   z\n   \\end{bmatrix}\n   =\n   \\begin{bmatrix}\n   0 & 8 & 0 \\\\\n   0 & 0 & -2 \\\\\n   2 & 8 & -2\n   \\end{bmatrix}\n   \\begin{bmatrix}\n   x \\\\\n   y \\\\\n   z\n   \\end{bmatrix}\n   $$\n\n2. **Find the eigenvalues of the coefficient matrix**:\n   The characteristic equation is:\n   $$\n   \\lambda^3 + 2\\lambda^2 + 16\\lambda + 32 = 0\n   $$\n   The roots are \\( \\lambda = -2, 4i, -4i \\).\n\n3. **Find the eigenvectors corresponding to each eigenvalue**:\n   - For \\( \\lambda = -2 \\), the eigenvector is \\( \\begin{bmatrix} -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\).\n   - For \\( \\lambda = 4i \\) and \\( \\lambda = -4i \\), the eigenvectors are complex conjugates, leading to real solutions involving sine and cosine terms.\n\n4. **Form the general solution**:\n   Using the eigenvalues and eigenvectors, the general solution is:\n   $$\n   \\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1 e^{-2t} \\begin{bmatrix} -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} + c_2 \\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\sin(4t) \\\\ \\cos(4t) \\\\ 2 \\sin(4t) \\end{bmatrix} + c_3 \\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\cos(4t) \\\\ \\sin(4t) \\\\ -2 \\cos(4t) \\end{bmatrix}\n   $$\n\n5. **Apply the initial conditions**:\n   Solving for \\( c_1, c_2, c_3 \\) using \\( x(0) = -4 \\), \\( y(0) = 0 \\), and \\( z(0) = 1 \\), we find:\n   - \\( c_1 = 1 \\)\n   - \\( c_2 = -1 \\)\n   - \\( c_3 = 0 \\)\n\n6. **Write the particular solution**:\n   Substituting the constants back into the general solution, we get:\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n