zed in terms of the exponent \\( E(n) \\) and shown to align with the formula after careful expansion and simplification.\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy induction and verification of initial terms, we conclude that the formula \\( \\left\\lfloor u_n \\right\\rfloor = 2^{\\frac{2^n - (-1)^n}{3}} \\) holds for all natural numbers \\( n \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2^{\\frac{2^n - (-1)^n}{3}}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We start by observing the terms of the sequence $\\left\\{u_{n}\\right\\}$. The initial terms are defined as follows:\n   \\[\n   u_{0}=2, \\quad u_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n   Using the provided recurrence relation:\n   \\[\n   u_{n+1}=u_{n}\\left(u_{n-1}^{2}-2\\right)-u_{1} \\quad \\text{for } n \\geq 1\n   \\]\n\n2. First, we verify the hypothesis for the initial terms given. We need to show that:\n   \\[\n   u_{n} = 2^{\\frac{2^n - (-1)^n}{3}} + 2^{-\\frac{2^n - (-1)^n}{3}}\n   \\]\n   is valid for $u_0$ and $u_1$.\n\n3. Check for \\( n = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   u_{0} = 2 = 2^{\\frac{2^0 - (-1)^0}{3}} + 2^{-\\frac{2^0 - (-1)^0}{3}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   2^{\\frac{1}{3}} + 2^{-\\frac{1}{3}} = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   Thus, the formula holds for \\( u_{0} \\).\n\n4. Check for \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   u_{1} = \\frac{5}{2} = 2^{\\frac{2^1 - (-1)^1}{3}} + 2^{-\\frac{2^1 - (-1)^1}{3}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{3} = 1,\\quad \\text{so} \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2^{\\frac{3}{3}} + 2^{-\\frac{3}{3}} = 2^1 + 2^{-1}= 2 + \\frac{1}{2} = 2.5\n   \\]\n   Thus, the formula holds for \\( u_{1} \\).\n\n5. Assuming the hypothesis holds for \\( u_{k-1} \\) and \\( u_{k} \\), we want to show it holds for \\( u_{k+1} \\). \n   By induction hypothesis:\n   \\[\n   u_{k-1} = 2^{f(k-1)} + 2^{-f(k-1)}, \\quad u_{k} = 2^{f(k)} + 2^{-f(k)}\n   \\]\n   where \\( f(n) = \\frac{2^n - (-1)^n}{3} \\).\n\n6. We now compute \\( u_{k+1} \\) using the recurrence relation:\n   \\[\n   u_{k+1} = u_{k} \\left(u_{k-1}^{2} - 2 \\right) - u_{1}\n   \\]\n   Substituting