al to \\(60^\\circ\\).\n   - A right triangle requires a \\(90^\\circ\\) angle, which cannot be at the vertices of the equilateral triangle.\n   - Midpoints of the sides of the equilateral triangle play a crucial role in forming right triangles with the vertices.\n\n2. **Right Triangles with Vertices and Midpoints**:\n   - Consider the midpoints of the sides of the triangle. For example, the midpoint \\(M\\) of \\(AC\\) forms a right triangle with vertices \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). This is verified by checking the vectors from \\(M\\) to \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), which are perpendicular.\n   - Similarly, midpoints of other sides form right triangles with the opposite vertices.\n\n3. **Pigeonhole Principle and Coloring**:\n   - By the pigeonhole principle, at least two of the vertices \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) must be the same color. Assume \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are red.\n   - The midpoint \\(M_1\\) of \\(AC\\) must be colored blue to avoid forming a red right triangle \\(ABM_1\\).\n   - If the third vertex \\(C\\) is red, the midpoint \\(M_2\\) of \\(BC\\) must be blue to avoid a red right triangle \\(ACM_2\\).\n   - Similarly, the midpoint \\(M_3\\) of \\(AB\\) must be considered. If \\(M_3\\) is red, it forms a right triangle with \\(B\\) and \\(C\\), leading to a contradiction if \\(C\\) is blue.\n\n4. **General Case and Continuum Argument**:\n   - The problem involves all points on the sides, not just the vertices and midpoints. Even if the midpoints are colored to avoid monochromatic right triangles with vertices, there are infinitely many other points on the sides.\n   - Using the density of points on each side and the Intermediate Value Theorem, we can argue that there must be a monochromatic right triangle formed by points on the sides, even if they are not vertices or midpoints.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - Regardless of how the points are colored, the structure of the equilateral triangle and the properties of right triangles ensure that at least one subset must contain a right triangle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At least one subset contains the vertices of a right triangle.}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's consider the equilateral triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n\n1. **Division of Points**: Take the points on the sides $AB, BC, AC$, including the vertices $A, B,$ and $C$. Divide these points into two non-overlapping subsets, say \\( S_1 \\) and \\( S_2 \\).\n\n2. **Contradiction Assumption**: Suppose that neither \\( S_1 \\) nor \\( S_2 \\) contains all three vertices of any right triangle. We will use proof by contradiction to demonstrate that at least one subset must include a right triangle.\n\n3. **Auxiliary Points**: Assume, for instance, in Figure 3-12, that \\( X, Y, \\) and \\( Z \\) are points on sides \\( AB, BC, \\) and \\( AC \\) respectively.\n\n4. **Ratio Consistency**:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AX}{XB} = \\frac{BY}{YC} = \\frac{CZ}{ZA} = 2\n    \\]\n   This ensures that \\( AX = \\frac{2}{3} AB \\), \\( BY = \\frac{2}{3} BC \\), and \\( CZ = \\frac{2}{3} CA \\).\n\n5. **Length Calculation**:\n    \\[\n    (XZ)^2 = (AX)^2 + (AZ)^2 - 2 \\cdot (AX) \\cdot (AZ) \\cdot \\cos(\\angle XAZ)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = \\left( \\frac{2}{3} AB \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} AC \\right)^2 - 2 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{3} AC \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{2}{3} AB \\right) \\cdot \\cos 60^\\circ\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^2 AB^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^2 AC^2 - 2 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\right) AB \\cdot AC \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = \\frac{4}{9} AB^2 + \\frac{1}{9} AC^2 - \\left( \\frac{2}{9} \\right) AB \\cdot AC \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = \\frac{4}{9} AB^2 + \\frac{1}{9} AC^2 - \\frac{1}{9} AB \\cdot AC\n    \\]\n    Note that in an equilateral triangle, \\( AB = AC \\), so:\n    \\[\n    = \\frac{4}{9} AB^2 + \\frac{1}{9} AB^2 - \\frac{1}{9} AB^2 \\cdot \\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{3} AB^2\n    \\]\n\n6. Proof of Right Triangle:\n    \\[\n    (XZ)^2 + (AZ)^2 = \\frac{1}{3} AB^2 + \\frac{1}{9} AC^2 = \\frac{4}{9} AB^2 = (AX)^2\n    \\]\n   Thus proving \\( \\angle AXZ = 90^\\circ \\). Likewise, for \\( \\triangle BYX \\) and \\( \\triangle CYZ \\):\n    \\[\n    \\angle BYC = \\angle CYZ = 90^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n7. **Subset Distribution**:\n    Points \\( X, Y, Z \\) must be distributed in such a way that at least two points belong to the same subset, either \\( S_1 \\) or \\( S_2 \\).\n\n    Assume, without loss of generality, that \\( \\{X, Y\\} \\subseteq S_1 \\). \n\n8. **Verification**:\n    \n    Given $XY$ is parallel to $AB$. From the earlier assumption that any three vertices cannot be in the same set:\n    \\[\n    AB \\text{ contains no other points from } S_1 \\text{ except for } X.\n    \\]\n\n    Any additional point \\( W \\in S_1 \\) on \\( AB \\) would mean \n    \\[\n    \\{X, Y, W\\} \\text{ forms a triangle with a 90-degree vertex in } S_1,\n    \\]\n    Contradicting our initial assumption.\n\nThus, the points on $AB$ that belong to \\( S_2 \\) do not include \\( C \\) on \\( S_2 \\).\n\n9. **Conclusion**:\n    If $C$ and $Z$ do not belong to \\( S_2 \\), they must belong to \\( S_1 \\).\n\n    Therefore, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\{C, Z, Y\\} \\subseteq S_1\n    \\]\n\n    This leads to the conclusion that:\n    \\( \\{C, Z, Y\\} \\) forms a right triangle, contradicting our contrarian assumption. \n\n### Conclusion:\nThus proving that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At least one of the subsets contains a right-angled triangle.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In space, there are \\(n\\) planes. Any 2 planes intersect in a line, any 3 planes intersect at a point, and no 4 planes intersect at a single point. How many non-overlapping regions do these \\(n\\) planes divide the space into?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many non-overlapping regions n planes divide the space in