2261 0 mod S or 1 + k\u00b3 \u2261 0 mod S. But abc \u2261 0 mod S is impossible because a, b, c, d, e, f are positive integers less than S, so abc can't be divisible by S. Therefore, 1 + k\u00b3 \u2261 0 mod S \u21d2 k\u00b3 \u2261 -1 mod S.\n\nFrom the second equation: ab + bc + ca - de - ef - fd \u2261 ab + bc + ca - k\u00b2(ab + bc + ca) \u2261 (1 - k\u00b2)(ab + bc + ca) \u2261 0 mod S. Again, since ab + bc + ca \u2261 0 mod S would imply that at least one of a, b, c is 0 mod S, which is impossible. Therefore, 1 - k\u00b2 \u2261 0 mod S \u21d2 k\u00b2 \u2261 1 mod S.\n\nSo, from the second equation, k\u00b2 \u2261 1 mod S \u21d2 k \u2261 \u00b11 mod S.\n\nFrom the first equation, k\u00b3 \u2261 -1 mod S.\n\nIf k \u2261 1 mod S, then k\u00b3 \u2261 1 mod S \u2260 -1 mod S. If k \u2261 -1 mod S, then k\u00b3 \u2261 (-1)\u00b3 \u2261 -1 mod S, which satisfies the first equation. Therefore, k \u2261 -1 mod S.\n\nThus, if such a k exists, then k = -1 mod S, which would imply that d \u2261 -a mod S, e \u2261 -b mod S, f \u2261 -c mod S.\n\nBut in that case, the sum S = a + b + c + d + e + f \u2261 a + b + c - a - b - c \u2261 0 mod S. Which is always true, but we need the actual sum S = a + b + c + d + e + f to be equal to S, so this is just an identity. However, the variables d, e, f are positive integers less than S, so d = S - a, e = S - b, f = S - c. But then d + e + f = 3S - (a + b + c). Then total sum S = (a + b + c) + (3S - (a + b + c)) = 3S \u21d2 S = 3S \u21d2 S=0, which is impossible. Hence, this leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, assuming that the two trios are proportional modulo S leads to a contradiction when S is prime. Therefore, such a proportionality is impossible. However, does this mean that no solutions exist when S is prime?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there are solutions where the trios are not proportional, but still satisfy the given conditions. However, if we can show that no such solutions exist when S is prime, then S must be composite.\n\nBut how to show that?\n\nAlternatively, consider the following: from the first equation, abc \u2261 -def mod S. Let me multiply both sides by (a + b + c):\n\nabc(a + b + c) \u2261 -def(a + b + c) mod S\n\nBut since S = a + b + c + d + e + f, then a + b + c = S - (d + e + f). Therefore:\n\nabc(S - (d + e + f)) \u2261 -def(S - (d + e + f)) mod S\n\nSimplify:\n\nabc S - abc(d + e + f) \u2261 -def S + def(d + e + f) mod S\n\nBut abc S \u2261 0 mod S and -def S \u2261 0 mod S, so we have:\n\n- abc(d + e + f) + def(d + e + f) \u2261 0 mod S\n\nFactor out (d + e + f):\n\n(d + e + f)(def - abc) \u2261 0 mod S\n\nBut S is prime, so either d + e + f \u2261 0 mod S or def - abc \u2261 0 mod S.\n\nIf d + e + f \u2261 0 mod S, then since d + e + f = S - (a + b + c), we have S - (a + b + c) \u2261 0 mod S \u21d2 -(a + b + c) \u2261 0 mod S \u21d2 a + b + c \u2261 0 mod S. But a + b + c is less than S (since d, e, f are at least 1), so a + b + c cannot be 0 mod S. Therefore, this case is impossible.\n\nThe other possibility is def - abc \u2261 0 mod S \u21d2 def \u2261 abc mod S. But from the first condition, abc \u2261 -def mod S. Combining these gives def \u2261 -def mod S \u21d2 2def \u2261 0 mod S.\n\nSince S is prime, this implies either S divides 2 or S divides def. If S divides 2, then S=2. But S is the sum of six positive integers, so minimum S=6, which is composite. Hence, S=2 is impossible. Otherwise, S divides def. But def is less than S^3, but since S is prime, S divides def implies that S divides at least one of d, e, or f. However, d, e, f are positive integers less than S (as part of the sum S = a + b + c + d + e + f). Hence, d, e, f < S, so S cannot divide any of them. Contradiction. Therefore, both cases lead to a contradiction, which implies that our initial assumption that S is prime is false. Therefore, S must be composite.\n\nThis seems to be the crux. Let me recap this argument to ensure its validity.\n\nStarting from the two conditions:\n\n1. abc + def \u2