rmula give [ c(4c\u00b2 +3b\u00b2 ) ]/[ b(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ] ?\n\nBut with c=1, b=2, that gives [1*(4 +12 )]/[2*(4 +4 ) ]=16/16=1, which is wrong. Therefore, mistake in the general case.\n\nLet me re-express the cross product and dot product.\n\nIn the general case:\n\nQM = ( -c(2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ), -b c\u00b2/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) )\n\nQP = ( -c b\u00b2/(2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ), -b c\u00b2/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) )\n\nCross product:\n\nQM_x * QP_y - QM_y * QP_x =\n\n[ -c(2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ] * [ -b c\u00b2/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ] - [ -b c\u00b2/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ] * [ -c b\u00b2/(2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ) ]\n\nFirst term:\n\n[ c(2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) * b c\u00b2 ] / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 = b c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2\n\nSecond term:\n\n[ b c\u00b2 * c b\u00b2 ] / [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ] = b\u00b3 c\u00b3 / [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ]\n\nThus, total cross product magnitude:\n\nb c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 - b\u00b3 c\u00b3 / [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ]\n\nWait, the second term has a minus sign in front due to the original expression:\n\n- [ -b c\u00b2/(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ] * [ -c b\u00b2/(2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ) ] = - [ b c\u00b2 * c b\u00b2 / (2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ) ] = - [ b\u00b3 c\u00b3 / (2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ) ]\n\nTherefore, the cross product is:\n\nFirst term (positive) + second term (negative):\n\nb c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 - b\u00b3 c\u00b3 / [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ]\n\nFactor out b c\u00b3 / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2:\n\n= b c\u00b3 / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 [ (2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) - (b\u00b2 ) / 2 ]\n\n= b c\u00b3 / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 [ 2c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -0.5b\u00b2 ]\n\n= b c\u00b3 / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 [ 2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ]\n\n= b c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2\n\nNow, the dot product QM \u00b7 QP was computed as (b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) / [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ]\n\nTherefore, tan(theta )= |cross product| / dot product\n\n= [ b c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ] / [ b\u00b2 c\u00b2 / (2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ) ]\n\n= [ b c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2 ] * [ 2(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) / (b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) ]\n\n= [ c (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) * 2 ] / [ b (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ]\n\n= [ 2c (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) ] / [ b (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ]\n\n= [ 4c\u00b3 + c b\u00b2 ] / [ b (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ]\n\nFactor numerator:\n\n= c(4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) / [ b (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 ) ]\n\n= c / b\n\nAh! Finally! So tan(theta )= c / b\n\nWhich matches tan(angle ABM )=c / b\n\nTherefore, angle ABM = angle MQP.\n\nSo the mistake was in the earlier cross product calculation where I incorrectly added the terms instead of subtracting. The correct cross product magnitude is b c\u00b3 (2c\u00b2 +0.5b\u00b2 ) / (4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2 )\u00b2, leading to tan(theta )=c / b.\n\nTherefore, in both cases, tan(angle ABM )=c / b and tan(angle MQP )=c / b, hence the angles are equal.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\(\\angle ABM = \\angle MQP\\) in triangle \\(ABC\\), we use coordinate geometry and vector analysis.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup:**\n   - Place \\(A\\) at \\((0, 0)\\), \\(B\\) at \\((0, b)\\), and \\(C\\) at \\((2c, 0)\\).\n   - Midpoint \\(M\\) of \\(AC\\) is \\((c, 0)\\).\n   - Midpoint \\(P\\) of \\(CM\\) is \\((1.5c, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABP\\):**\n   - Points \\(A(0,0)\\), \\(B(0,b)\\), \\(P(1.5c, 0)\\).\n   - Equation of the circumcircle: \\(x^2 + y^2 - 1.5cx - by = 0\\).\n\n3. **Equation of Line \\(BC\\):**\n   - Slope of \\(BC\\): \\(-\\frac{b}{2c}\\).\n   - Equation: \\(y = -\\frac{b}{2c}x + b\\).\n\n4. **Find Intersection \\(Q\\):**\n   - Solve the system of the circle and line \\(BC\\).\n   - After substitution and simplification, coordinates of \\(Q\\) are: