to end up in the reverse order (white, blue, red) after the 100th round because such a configuration requires an odd permutation, while all possible rearrangements (derangements) are even permutations.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Problem Overview:**\n   - We start with three differently colored balls initially ordered as: Red, Blue, White.\n   - The task is to determine whether it's possible to rearrange these balls into the reverse order: White, Blue, Red, after 100 rounds of swaps, where in each round no ball stays in its current position.\n\n2. **Possible Permutations:**\n   - We first identify the possible valid permutations of the balls after any number of rounds. These permutations will always ensure that no ball remains in its initial position.\n   - The initial order is Red, Blue, White (RBW).\n\n3. **Valid Permutations under Given Constraints:**\n   - Under the problem constraints, the allowed permutations where no ball remains in its place are:\n     1. (Red, Blue, White) -> Initial state (RBW)\n     2. (Blue, White, Red) -> First valid transition (BWR)\n     3. (White, Red, Blue) -> Second valid transition (WRB)\n   - Any configuration of the three balls must be one of these three to satisfy the given condition that no ball remains in place after a swap.\n\n4. **Desired Final Permutation:**\n   - We aim to see if it's possible to reach the permutation (White, Blue, Red) which is the reverse order of the initial state.\n\n5. **Checking Feasibility:**\n   - Let's list the valid permutations:\n     - (Red, Blue, White) -> Initial\n     - (Blue, White, Red)\n     - (White, Red, Blue)\n   - Among these permutations, (White, Blue, Red) is not present in the list. This confirms that one cannot achieve this specific order no matter how many rounds of swaps are performed.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since (White, Blue, Red) is not among the valid permutations, it is impossible for the balls to be in that configuration after 100 rounds or any number of rounds under the given rules.\n\n$\\boxed{\\text{It is not possible for the balls to lie in the order White, Blue, Red after 100 rounds.}}$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A chocolate egg manufacturer has announced a new collection called \"Nile Family,\" which includes ten different charming crocodiles. The crocodiles are uniformly and randomly distributed among the chocolate eggs, meaning each crocodile can be found in any randomly chosen egg with a probability of 0.1.\n\nTwins Lyosha and Gosha want to collect two sets\u2014one for each brother. Every day, their mother buys them one egg each. At some point, the first collection is finally completed.\n\nLet \\( p_{k} \\) be the probability that at the moment the first collection is completed, exactly \\( k \\) crocodiles are missing from the second collection.\n\na) (For Grade 7, 3 points) Prove that \\( p_{1} = p_{2} \\).\n\nb) (For Grade 8, 4 points) Prove that: \\( p_{2} > p_{3} > p_{4} > \\ldots > p_{10} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem about the Nile Family crocodile collection. It's part a and part b. Let's start with part a: Prove that p\u2081 = p\u2082. \n\nFirst, I need to understand what p\u2096 represents. The problem says that p\u2096 is the probability that when the first collection is completed, exactly k crocodiles are missing from the second collection. So, when one brother has all 10 crocodiles, the other brother is missing k. So, the twins are collecting two sets, each day their mom buys them one egg each. So each day, each brother gets a crocodile, right? And the crocodiles are uniformly distributed with probability 0.1 for each of the 10 types. \n\nSo, the process continues until one of the brothers has completed his collection. At that point, we look at the other brother's collection and see how many are missing. The question is asking for the probability that exactly 1 is missing (p\u2081) and exactly 2 are missing (p\u2082) and show they're equal.\n\nHmm. So, maybe when the first collection is completed, the second collection has 10 - k unique crocodiles. So, missing k. So, we need to compute the probability that when the first brother completes his collection (has all 10), the second brother is missing exactly k. Then, they want to show that for k=1 and k=2, these probabilities are equal.\n\nLet me think. To approach this, perhaps I can model the problem as a coupon collector problem. The standard coupon collector problem deals with the number of trials needed to collect all coupons. But here, we have two collectors in parallel, each getting a coupon each day. The process stops when one of them completes their collection. Then, we need to find the distribution of the number of coupons the other has when this happens.\n\nLet me formalize this. Let\u2019s denote that each day, two coupons (crocodiles) are acquired, one by each brother. Each coupon is independently chosen with equal probability 1/10 for each type. Let\u2019s say we track the progress of both brothers over time. Let T be the time (number of days) when the first brother completes his collection. At time T, we look at the second brother's collection and count how many coupons are missing. The claim is that the probability that exactly 1 is missing (p\u2081) is equal to the probability that exactly 2 are missing (p\u2082).\n\nTo show this, perhaps there's a symmetry or combinatorial argument. Let me think about the possible configurations when the first collection is completed. When the first brother gets his last needed coupon, the second brother has some number of coupons. The key is that the last coupon that completed the first brother's collection could have been a duplicate for the second brother or not. Wait, but each day, they get separate coupons. So, on each day, each brother gets a coupon independently. So, when the first brother completes his collection, that is, when he obtains his 10th uniqu