omes f(y) + f(y) + f(0) \u2265 3f(2y). So 2f(y) + f(0) \u2265 3f(2y). Interesting. Maybe this can lead us somewhere. Let's note this down as equation (1): 2f(y) + f(0) \u2265 3f(2y).\n\nAlternatively, maybe set y = 0. Let's try y = 0. Then the original inequality becomes f(x) + f(z) + f(z + x) \u2265 3f(x + 3z). Hmm, not sure if that helps yet. Let me think.\n\nAnother approach: Suppose f is linear, maybe f(t) = at + b. Let's test if such functions satisfy the inequality. Let's substitute f(t) = at + b into the original inequality.\n\nCompute left-hand side (LHS): f(x+y) + f(y+z) + f(z+x) = a(x+y) + b + a(y+z) + b + a(z+x) + b = a(2x + 2y + 2z) + 3b.\n\nRight-hand side (RHS): 3f(x + 2y + 3z) = 3[a(x + 2y + 3z) + b] = 3a(x + 2y + 3z) + 3b.\n\nSo the inequality becomes a(2x + 2y + 2z) + 3b \u2265 3a(x + 2y + 3z) + 3b.\n\nSubtract 3b from both sides: 2a(x + y + z) \u2265 3a(x + 2y + 3z).\n\nBring all terms to one side: 2a(x + y + z) - 3a(x + 2y + 3z) \u2265 0.\n\nFactor out a: a[2(x + y + z) - 3(x + 2y + 3z)] \u2265 0.\n\nCompute the expression inside the brackets: 2x + 2y + 2z - 3x -6y -9z = (-x -4y -7z).\n\nSo the inequality becomes a(-x -4y -7z) \u2265 0 for all x, y, z \u2208 \u211d.\n\nBut since x, y, z can be any real numbers, the expression (-x -4y -7z) can take any real value. For the inequality a*(-x -4y -7z) \u2265 0 to hold for all x, y, z, the coefficient a must be zero. Otherwise, if a \u2260 0, we can choose x, y, z such that (-x -4y -7z) is positive or negative, which would make the left-hand side positive or negative depending on a's sign. Thus, the only linear function possible is a constant function, where a = 0. Then f(t) = b.\n\nCheck if constant functions work: If f is constant, f(t) = b for all t. Then LHS = 3b, RHS = 3b. So 3b \u2265 3b, which holds as equality. Therefore, constant functions are solutions. Hmm, so at least constant functions satisfy the inequality. Maybe they are the only solutions? Need to check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe quadrati