  \\(\\lacksquare\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "16 teams participate in a round-robin football tournament. Prove that at any given moment in the tournament, there are two teams that have played the same number of matches (possibly, 0 matches).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this problem. So, there's a round-robin football tournament with 16 teams. We need to prove that at any given moment during the tournament, there are two teams that have played the same number of matches. Hmm, interesting. Alright, let's start breaking this down.\n\nFirst, let's recall what a round-robin tournament is. In such a tournament, each team plays every other team exactly once, right? But the problem says \"at any given moment,\" which means we're considering the tournament while it's ongoing, not after it's completed. So, not all matches have been played yet. The key point is that even during the tournament, before all matches are finished, there must be two teams with the same number of games played.\n\nSo, how do we approach this? Maybe using the Pigeonhole Principle? That's often used in problems where we have to show that some condition must hold because there are more \"pigeons\" than \"holes.\" Let me think.\n\nEach team can have played anywhere from 0 to 15 matches because there are 15 other teams. Wait, but since the tournament is ongoing, the maximum number of matches any team has played could be less than 15. However, at any point in time, each team has played some number of matches between 0 and 15. But here's the thing: if we consider the number of possible distinct values for the number of matches played, that's 16 possibilities (0 through 15). But there are 16 teams. So, if each team had a different number of matches played, then all numbers from 0 to 15 would be represented. But is that possible?\n\nWait a second. Let me think. Suppose we try to assign each team a distinct number of matches played. So one team has 0, another has 1, all the way up to 15. But there's a problem here. If one team has played 0 matches, that means it hasn't played against any other team. But the team that supposedly has played 15 matches must have played against every other team, including the one that has played 0 matches. But that's a contradiction, right? Because if the team with 15 matches played has played against everyone else, then the team with 0 matches played must have at least played that one match against the team with 15. So, it's impossible for both a team with 0 matches and a team with 15 matches to exist simultaneously.\n\nAh! So that means that the maximum and minimum number of matches can't both be present. Therefore, the possible number of matches any team can have is either from 0 to 14 or from 1 to 15. So, in either case, there are only 15 possible distinct values. Since there are 16 teams, by the Pigeonhole Principle, at least two teams must have the same number of matches played.\n\nWait, let me verify this again. If there's a team that has played 0 matches, then no team can have played 15 matches. Conversely, if a team has played 15 matches, then all other teams have played at least 1 match. Therefore, the number of distinct possible values for the number of matches played is actually 15, not 16. Hence, with 16 teams, two must share the same number. That seems right.\n\nLet me formalize this argument. Suppose for contradiction that all teams have distinct numbers of matches played. The possible number of matches is from 0 to 15. However, if one team has 0 matches, then no team can have 15 matches, because that team would have had to play against the team with 0 matches. Similarly, if one team has 15 matches, then all other teams have at least 1 match, so 0 is excluded. Therefore, the possible number of matches is either 0 to 14 or 1 to 15, each giving 15 possible values. But there are 16 teams, so by Pigeonhole Principle, two teams must have the same number. Therefore, the original assumption is false, and such two teams must exist.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete. The key idea is recognizing that the maximum and minimum possible number of matches can't coexist, thereby reducing the number of possible distinct values by one. Then, since the number of teams exceeds the number of possible distinct values, two teams must share the same number of matches played. \n\nI should check if there's another angle. Maybe graph theory? Since a tournament can be represented as a graph where each node is a team and each edge is a match. The degree of each node corresponds to the number of matches played. So, the problem reduces to showing that in any subgraph of the complete graph on 16 nodes, there are two nodes with the same degree. \n\nIn a complete graph, each node has degree 15. But we're considering an incomplete graph since the tournament is ongoing. The degrees can range from 0 to 15, but as before, 0 and 15 can't both be present. Hence, degrees are in either 0-14 or 1-15, both 15 possibilities. So, 16 nodes must have at least two with the same degree. Yep, same conclusion.\n\nIs there a case where even with 16 teams and 15 possible degrees, you could still have all degrees unique? The contradiction comes from 0 and 15 being mutually exclusive, so you can't have both, hence only 15 possible degrees. Therefore, two teams must share. That seems solid.\n\nI don't see any flaws in this reasoning. Let me think of an example. Suppose in the beginning, all teams have 0 matches. Trivially, all have the same number. Once some matches start, but as long as not all teams have played different numbers, there could be duplicates. But according to the proof, even if you tried to arrange the matches so that each team has a unique number of games, you can't because of the 0 and 15 conflict. Therefore, duplicates are inevitable. \n\nAnother way: if all degrees are unique, then the degree sequence would have to be 0,1,2,...,15. 