nal equation yields:\n   \\[\n   3(z_i - w_1)(z_i - w_2) = (z_i - z_j)(z_i - z_k)\n   \\]\n   Taking magnitudes:\n   \\[\n   3|z_i - w_1||z_i - w_2| = |z_i - z_j||z_i - z_k|\n   \\]\n\n3. **Contradiction via Product Bounds:**\n   Assume both \\(|z_i - w_1| > 1\\) and \\(|z_i - w_2| > 1\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   |z_i - w_1||z_i - w_2| > 1 \\implies |z_i - z_j||z_i - z_k| > 3\n   \\]\n   However, since \\(|z_i - z_j|, |z_i - z_k| \\leq 2\\) (as all \\(z\\) are in the unit circle), their product \\(|z_i - z_j||z_i - z_k| \\leq 4\\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|z_i - z_j||z_i - z_k|}{3} \\leq \\frac{4}{3} < 3\n   \\]\n   This contradicts \\(|z_i - z_j||z_i - z_k| > 3\\), proving our assumption false.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, for each \\(z_i\\), at least one root \\(w_1\\) or \\(w_2\\) must satisfy \\(|w - z_i| \\leq 1\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\(\\boxed{\\min \\{\\left|w_{1}-z_{i}\\right|,\\left|w_{2}-z_{i}\\right|\\} \\leq 1}\\) for \\(i=1,2,3\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo prove that \\(\\min \\left\\{ \\left| w_1 - z_i \\right|, \\left| w_2 - z_i \\right| \\right\\} \\leq 1 \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, 3 \\), given that the moduli of the complex numbers \\(z_1, z_2, z_3\\) are not greater than 1, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Minimization Requirement**:\n   Due to the symmetry of the problem, it suffices to show:\n   \\[\n   \\min \\left\\{ \\left| w_1 - z_1 \\right|, \\left| w_2 - z_1 \\right| \\right\\} \\leq 1\n   \\]\n\n2. **Properties from Vieta's Theorem**:\n   From Vieta's formulas for the roots \\(w_1\\) and \\(w_2\\) of the given polynomial, we have:\n   \\[\n   w_1 + w_2 = \\frac{2(z_1 + z_2 + z_3)}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   w_1 w_2 = \\frac{z_1 z_2 + z_2 z_3 + z_3 z_1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. From these, we deduce:\n   \\[\n   (w_1 - z_1) + (w_2 - z_1) = \\frac{2[(z_2 - z_1) + (z_3 - z_1)]}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (w_1 - z_1)(w_2 - z_1) = \\frac{(z_2 - z_1) \\cdot (z_3 - z_1)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Ratio Calculation**:\n   Dividing the two equations above, we get