quation: y - 0.5 = -1(x - 0) \u2192 y = -x + 0.5.\n\nThis line passes through (0.25,0.25), so it's below the center (1,1). This cut removes a triangle from the lower-left corner.\n\nAfter four cuts, we've removed triangles from all four corners. The remaining pie is an octagon. The center is still inside. Maybe by making more such cuts, we can get closer.\n\nBut how does this help in isolating the raisin? Each cut removes a corner, but the center remains. The remaining area becomes more circular, but still, each new cut is from two adjacent sides of the current polygon. Wait, but the problem states that each cut must be along a line intersecting two adjacent sides of the original square. Wait, no, the problem says \"along a line intersecting two adjacent sides at points different from the vertices\". Does this mean that the two adjacent sides must be sides of the original square, or can they be sides of the remaining pie?\n\nThis is crucial. If the cuts must intersect two adjacent sides of the original square, then even after cutting off some triangles, the subsequent cuts still have to go from two adjacent original sides. But if the cuts can intersect two adjacent sides of the current remaining pie (which might have more sides), then the strategy changes.\n\nRe-reading the problem: \"cut a triangular piece from the pie along a line intersecting two adjacent sides at points different from the vertices\". It doesn't specify whether the sides are of the original square or the current pie. So likely, it refers to the original square's sides. Otherwise, if it refers to the current pie's sides, after the first cut, the pie has five sides, and adjacent sides would be different.\n\nBut the problem says \"the pie\" which is being cut. Initially, the pie is a square. After each cut, the remaining pie is a polygon. So when it says \"intersecting two adjacent sides\", it refers to the current pie's sides. So after the first cut, the pie is a pentagon, and the next cut would be