e equal to (S - i - j)/3. So we can write:\n\nSum from i+1 to j-1 = (S - i - j)/3.\n\nBut the sum from i+1 to j-1 is equal to Sum from 1 to j-1 - Sum from 1 to i = [ (j-1)j / 2 ] - [ i(i+1)/2 ].\n\nTherefore:\n\n[ (j-1)j / 2 - i(i+1)/2 ] = (S - i - j)/3\n\nMultiply both sides by 6 to eliminate denominators:\n\n3(j-1)j - 3i(i+1) = 2(S - i - j)\n\nWhich again gives the same equation. So regardless of how we approach it, we end up with the same equation.\n\nGiven that, perhaps we can rearrange terms to make it easier. Let's write:\n\n3j^2 - 3j - 3i^2 - 3i - 2S + 2i + 2j = 0\n\nWait, 3j^2 - 3j - 3i^2 - 3i = 2S - 2i - 2j\n\nSo bringing all to left:\n\n3j^2 - 3j - 3i^2 - 3i - 2S + 2i + 2j = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n3j^2 - j - 3i^2 - i - 2S = 0\n\nSo 3j^2 - j - 3i^2 - i = 2S\n\nWe know S = 2,037,171, so 2S = 4,074,342. Therefore:\n\n3j^2 - j - 3i^2 - i = 4,074,342\n\nHmm. Maybe factor this equation. Let's see:\n\n3j^2 - j - 3i^2 - i = 4,074,342\n\nFactor terms with j and i:\n\n3(j^2 - i^2) - (j + i) = 4,074,342\n\nNote that j^2 - i^2 = (j - i)(j + i). Therefore:\n\n3(j - i)(j + i) - (j + i) = 4,074,342\n\nFactor (j + i):\n\n(j + i)(3(j - i) - 1) = 4,074,342\n\nSo, we have:\n\n(j + i)(3(j - i) - 1) = 4,074,342\n\nThis is interesting. Let me denote:\n\nLet\u2019s let s = j + i and d = j - i. Then, since j > i, d is a positive integer, and s and d are positive integers with s > d. Then the equation becomes:\n\ns*(3d - 1) = 4,074,342\n\nTherefore, s and (3d - 1) are positive integer factors of 4,074,342. Moreover, since s = i + j and d = j - i, we can express i and j in terms of s and d:\n\nAdding the equations s = i + j and d = j - i, we get:\n\nj = (s + d)/2\n\ni = (s - d)/2\n\nTherefore, s and d must have the same parity (both even or both odd) so that i and j are integers.\n\nSo, s and d must satisfy:\n\n1. s * (3d - 1) = 4,074,342\n\n2. s > d\n\n3. s and d have the same parity.\n\nTherefore, to find possible pairs (s, d), we can factorize 4,074,342 into two factors s a